text:
Wiring
Members get the tool from swarm_tools(), which gives a member the tools of the swarm’s channels. Call it inside a member builder registered with @agent, and pass the builder to member() without calling it:
from inspect_ai.agent import Agent, agent, deepagent, react
from inspect_ai.tool import bash

from inspect_swarm import (
    filesystem,
    member,
    shared_memory,
    swarm,
    swarm_tools,
)


@agent
def solver_member() -&gt; Agent:
    return react(tools=[bash(), swarm_tools()])


@agent
def reviewer_member() -&gt; Agent:
    return deepagent(tools=[bash(), swarm_tools()])


solver = swarm(
    members=[
        member(solver_member, name="solver", count=3),
        member(reviewer_member, name="reviewer"),
    ],
    channels=[filesystem(), shared_memory()],
)

The log records a builder by name and arguments, so the swarm can be rebuilt from the log. It cannot rebuild swarm_tools() passed in args or inside an agent you built (Members).
Any agent that takes a ToolSource in its tools can take swarm_tools(). It gives no tools when the swarm has no channel with tools, or outside a swarm, so one member definition serves every arm of an experiment.
This swarm keeps the filesystem channel beside the board. channels=[shared_memory()], or channels=["shared_memory"], replaces it: members still share the sample’s sandbox, but their preamble no longer mentions /shared. Members with their own sandboxes can use the board with no shared volume.
The channel needs member names that start with a lowercase letter, followed by at most 47 lowercase letters, digits, _ or -.
Bridged members cannot use the channel yet, and swarm() refuses a bridged member beside it.

Monitoring with Inspect Sentinel
A native member’s call to shared_memory is an ordinary tool call, so Inspect Sentinel’s tool stages see it with no swarm code. A protocol at BeforeToolCall can reject a write before it reaches the board, and one at AfterToolCall sees exactly what a view is about to show. For example, a rule that keeps credentials off the board:
from inspect_sentinel import (
    BeforeToolCall,
    Context,
    Decision,
    Escalations,
    Protocol,
    call_text,
    find_words,
    protocol,
)


@protocol
def no_secrets_on_board() -&gt; Protocol:
    """Reject shared_memory writes that mention a credential."""

    async def decide(
        context: Context, step: BeforeToolCall, escalations: Escalations
    ) -&gt; Decision | None:
        if step.call.function != "shared_memory":
            return None
        if step.call.arguments.get("command") == "view":
            return None
        used = find_words(call_text(step.call), ["password", "api_key", "token"])
        if used:
            return Decision.reject(
                f"write mentions {', '.join(used)}",
                message="Credentials may not be written to shared memory.",
            )
        return Decision.proceed()

    return decide
Pass it to the task as Task(..., sentinel=no_secrets_on_board()). The member gets the decision’s message as the tool call’s error. A step’s conversation is the agent span id of the loop that made the call, and each comm event names that loop beside its member, so the two can be joined. Sentinel does not run on bridged members’ tool calls, which is one reason bridged members cannot use the channel yet.

Writing a channel
A channel is a factory decorated with @swarm_channel that returns a Channel. A channel that only adds instructions, as filesystem does, subclasses Channel and overrides instructions():
from inspect_swarm import Channel, MemberInfo, swarm_channel


class LeadChannel(Channel):
    def __init__(self, lead: str) -&gt; None:
        self.lead = lead

    def instructions(self, member: MemberInfo) -&gt; str | None:
        if member.name == self.lead:
            return "Write the plan to /shared/plan.md before you start."
        return f"Read /shared/plan.md, which {self.lead} writes, before you start."


@swarm_channel
def lead(name: str) -&gt; Channel:
    """Tell members that one of them writes a plan for the others."""
    return LeadChannel(name)
instructions() returns text for the member’s preamble, or None to add nothing. check(), if overridden, is called with the roster when swarm() is called and raises ValueError for a roster the channel cannot serve. A Channel holds configuration only: one object serves every sample. The factory’s arguments are logged with the channel.
A channel with tools and records, as shared_memory is, also sets name and prefix and returns a ChannelState from open() for each run of the swarm; its records go through the swarm’s bus. Its reach decides which of a member’s loops get its tools from swarm_tools(): "loop" (the default), only the member’s own loop; "member", also the nested loops the member’s agent gives swarm_tools(). The agent decides which nested loops get swarm_tools(); reach filters the channel tools they get and which channels can wake their swarm_wait. A channel whose reads consume something for the member, such as an inbox that marks messages read, keeps "loop", so that a subagent cannot read what the member’s own loop then never sees. shared_memory sets "member": a view consumes nothing, and each loop keeps its own seen state. A channel’s tools are given to the member as they are: with the messages channel, their results do not count the member’s unread messages, as the swarm’s own tools do. A member learns of messages from those, or by calling list_members. See the docstrings of Channel, ChannelState and WaitingState in the reference.

Shared
With no sandbox= on any member, members share the sample’s sandbox. The default filesystem channel gives them a convention for it: /shared for shared files, /shared/notes.md for notes, and /scratch/&lt;member&gt; for each member’s own work (Channels). The channel does not create these directories, so create them in the sandbox image or with the sample’s files. When members choose their own names (Members), create /shared and /scratch but not the members’ directories in it: a directory named after a member would tell members the swarm’s names.
examples/shared_sandbox.py runs bash() members in one container:
@task
def shared_sandbox(k: int = 3) -&gt; Task:
    return Task(
        dataset=[Sample(input=PROMPT, target="9", files={"/shared/notes.md": ""})],
        solver=swarm(
            members=member(react, args={"tools": [bash(timeout=60)]}, count=k),
            controller=leaderless(stop_on_submission=False),
        ),
        scorer=script_works(),
        sandbox=("docker", "compose.yaml"),
        token_limit=1_000_000,
    )
Members’ processes run side by side in one container, and nothing stops one member from killing another’s process or filling the disk. Keep the task’s checks and secrets out of a shared sandbox the members could read.
